.\" Manpage for puravida.
.TH PURAVIDA 1 "2026-06-14" "puravida 2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
puravida \- create files and directories in one command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B puravida
.RI [ path "...]"
.br
.B puravida
.I dir
.RI [ path "...]"
.br
.B puravida
.I file
.B ~~
.RI [ content "...]"
.br
.B puravida
.I file
.B ~
.br
.B puravida
.B \-f
.RI [ name "...]"
.br
.B puravida \-\-help
.br
.B puravida \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B puravida
is a one\-liner replacement for
.BR mkdir (1)
and
.BR touch (1),
and a cleaner alternative to here\-documents for writing text into a file.
Parent directories are created as needed.
.PP
Each argument is a path. Whether it is created as a file or a directory is
decided by its final path segment: if that segment contains a period (for
example
.IR notes.txt )
it is a file; otherwise it is a directory. A period in a parent segment is fine,
so
.I my.dir/notes
still creates
.I my.dir
as a directory. A trailing
.B /
always forces a directory, even when the leaf contains a period
.RI ( my.bak/ ).
.PP
If the first argument is a directory, the paths named after it are created
.I inside
it (with their own parent directories made as needed). If the first argument is
a file, every argument is created independently in the current directory, so
.I puravida a.txt b.txt
creates two files.
.PP
There is no
.B \-d
flag: a trailing
.B /
already forces a directory inline and composably (on any argument, not just the
first), which makes a separate flag redundant. Only the opposite case \(em
forcing a dotless name to be a file \(em needs the
.B \-f
flag, because there is no inline marker for it.
.PP
Inline content is introduced by
.BR ~~ :
everything after the first
.B ~~
is written, joined with spaces, into the most recently named file. Shells pass
.B ~~
through literally because it is not a valid tilde\-expansion. Content is written
verbatim, so quoting follows your shell's normal rules: single\-quote anything
containing
.BR $ ,
quotes, or spaces.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file
Treat dotless names as files (for example
.IR Makefile " or " LICENSE ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B puravida src/utils/helpers.js
Create the nested directories and an empty file.
.TP
.B puravida a.txt b.txt
Create two empty files.
.TP
.B puravida .claude/ settings.json commands/cmd.md
Create
.I .claude
containing
.I settings.json
and
.IR commands/cmd.md .
.TP
.B puravida -f Makefile LICENSE
Create two dotless files.
.TP
.B puravida global.css ~~ \(aq@import \(dqtailwindcss\(dq;\(aq
Create
.I global.css
containing the import line verbatim \(em the double quotes and semicolon are
preserved because the content is single\-quoted.
.TP
.B puravida notes.txt ~
Create
.I notes.txt
from pasted input; finish by entering a line containing only
.BR ~ .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime error (for example, a file or directory could not be created).
.TP
.B 2
A usage error (no arguments, an unknown option, or
.B ~~
with no preceding file).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR touch (1)
